--- a/speaking/resurrection-jesus-is-king/reading.html
+++ b/speaking/resurrection-jesus-is-king/reading.html
@@ -69,9 +69,9 @@
 <p>Easy irreversible damage. That’s the idea. It’s not hard to break pottery. You can do it accidentally if you aren’t careful. And God is giving Jesus a rod of iron. When you break pottery, there’s no putting it back together. It shatters. If the world is a china shop and the wicked rulers are the delicate dishes vainly displayed, God gave Jesus a baseball bat.</p>
 <p>This changes everything in the fight. We don’t know if we will live or we will die. We don’t know if we will win or we will lose in our lifetime. God may have awful judgment in store for our nation and things won't take a turn for the better (he knows we sure deserve it). But it changes everything to know that on your side—dwelling inside you, gathered here with us today by his Spirit—the risen Lord Jesus has all authority in heaven and on earth. With Jesus raised from the dead and his promise that he’ll raise us too, I’m happy to die in the fight. I’m happy to be a public spectacle. I can count it all joy when I face trials; I can count myself honored to be counted worthy to suffer for the name of Jesus as one of his slaves. Jesus is alive! He's already won.</p>
 <p>Church, don't fight like Easter never happened. Don't build like Easter came and went with no change. Jesus took the throne. Everything changed. He is our living hope—meaning that since he’s alive we have hope to face any trial or enemy. In WWII the Allied troops defeated the Axis powers at D-Day. It wasn’t until VE Day eleven months later that the war was really over, but everything really did change after D-Day. With the Axis power broken, don’t you think soldiers fought differently? It’s not that all risk was gone. It’s not that all the fighting was done and they could go home. But there was a new sure hope of victory that changed everything. That's what Easter should do for us. Victory is sure. Just keep marching on the side of King Jesus.</p>
-<h2>Serve The LORD And Take Refuge In Jesus (Vv. 10–12)</h2>
+<h2>Serve the Lord and Take Refuge in Jesus (vv. 10–12)</h2>
 <p>And now we come to the "so what?" The "therefore," the "how then shall we live" section of Psalm 2. The last stanza gives a warning and a promise.</p>
-<h2>Serve The LORD With Fear</h2>
+<h2>Serve the Lord with Fear</h2>
 <p>The warning is that the kings of earth better serve the Son—or else they're goners. Their plotting was in vain because God is more powerful than they. He's given the nations they rule to Jesus, so they had better get with the program and pay homage to Jesus. They had better serve the LORD with fear by submitting themselves to his anointed king instead of plotting to overthrow him. "Kiss the son" here isn't anything romantic; it's about reverential honor—like a duke kissing the ring of the king as he enters his presence.</p>
 <p>After Resurrection Sunday, everyone has two choices. They can submit to Jesus and be blessed by his Lordship, or they can rebel against Jesus and be shattered by his Lordship. This is what Easter is all about. The Son of God is so strong he wrestled death and won. There is no defeating him. The wisdom of God ran circles around the "wisdom" of man so thoroughly that at the very moment they thought they'd won they'd actually fallen on their own swords. There is no outwitting, out-maneuvering, or defeating God or his Son. You can either fall on your knees and pledge your life to him—heart, soul, mind, and strength—or you can fall on your knees at the end of time when Christ returns and regret you didn't do it sooner.</p>
 <p>Pastor Andy has said that there will be a right side and a wrong side of history.[3] And the only way to be on the right side of history is to be on Jesus' side. So, brothers and sisters, be warned. Don't resist our Lord. God is not mocked. He always has the last laugh. That’s why we are told to rejoice and <em>tremble.</em></p>
